# Line endings are LF in the index AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (T-151, session 62). This repo is developed on Windows with
# `core.autocrlf=true` and had no `.gitattributes`. That combination commits LF
# and checks out CRLF, so:
#
#   - a FRESH CLONE failed two assertions in `tests/pipelines/state-views.test.ts`
#     — `applySummaryRegion` expects `'# Project Summary\n\n'` and a freshly
#     checked-out fixture gave it `'\r\n\r\n'`;
#   - the long-lived working tree PASSED, because its fixtures were written LF
#     once and never checked out again;
#   - CI PASSED, because it runs on Linux.
#
# The suite was green in every place anyone looked and red in a fresh clone on
# the platform the project is developed on. It surfaced only when Loop 11 made
# the repo clonable with its instructions for the first time and someone
# actually cloned it.
#
# It cost twice more the same day: a raw `md5` comparison across the
# tracked/untracked boundary reported 7 of 11 commands "diverged" when none had
# (CR-only difference), and every commit printed "LF will be replaced by CRLF".
#
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text=auto` is deliberate: `text=auto` alone still
# defers the working-tree ending to `core.autocrlf`, which is the setting that
# caused this. This makes the checkout deterministic regardless of local config.
#
# Every tracked file here is text (.ts, .md, .json, .mjs, .yml) and the index is
# already LF, so this changes no blob — only what lands on disk.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the types actually in the tree, so a future binary addition is a
# deliberate decision rather than a silent match against `*`.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

